---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { Code } from 'astro-expressive-code/components';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import Callout from '../components/Callout.astro';
import HelpFooter from '../components/HelpFooter.astro';
import TrackNav from '../components/TrackNav.astro';
import { DAY2_DONE } from '../data/day2-done';
import { inlineMd } from '../lib/inline-md';

// Day 2 of the two-day training, written for the participant. The block order,
// times and "done when" lines follow the instructor curriculum (/schulung, Day 2);
// this page links to the detail pages rather than repeating them. The end-of-day
// checklist is shared data (src/data/day2-done.ts), not a second copy.

const MCP_ADD =
  'claude mcp add --transport http storybook-local http://localhost:<port>/mcp';
const SNAPSHOT_AND_CHECK = `npm run figma:snapshot
npm run check:contracts`;
const EMIT = 'npm run check:contracts -- --emit <folder>';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Build your own component (Day 2)"
  description="Day 2 of the Atelier training, for participants: choose a component brief, design it in Figma, write the handoff, generate contract, component and stories, iterate, verify and finish with a checklist."
  currentPath="/build-your-own"
  tocItems={[
    { id: 'goal', label: 'What you will have' },
    { id: 'block-00', label: '00 · Choose your brief' },
    { id: 'block-01', label: '01 · Design and handoff' },
    { id: 'block-02', label: '02 · Contract, component, stories' },
    { id: 'block-03', label: '03 · Iterate' },
    { id: 'block-04', label: '04 · Verify' },
    { id: 'block-05', label: '05 · Show and transfer' },
    { id: 'done', label: 'Are you done?' },
  ]}
>
  <div class="docs-inline-page">
    <PageHero
      time="Day 2 · 09:00–17:30"
      title="Build your own component"
      titleAccent="own"
      ledeMax="620px"
    >
      Day 1 you ran the loop on a component we gave you. Today you run it on one
      you design yourself: a brief, a Figma component set, a handoff document,
      then contract, component and stories, iterated until every check is green.
      You prompt; you do not type the code.
    </PageHero>

    <section class="docs-section" aria-labelledby="goal">
      <h2 class="docs-section-title" id="goal">What you will have</h2>
      <ul class="byo-list">
        <li>
          An own component in your own Figma draft: a component set with
          variants, properties and dark mode
        </li>
        <li>
          Contract, component and stories in your own workspace, in your
          framework
        </li>
        <li>An a11y audit passed, and dark mode working</li>
      </ul>
      <Callout tone="tip" title="Keep these open all day">
        <p>
          The <strong>Course kit</strong> in the sidebar links the pages you
          reach for most:
          <a href="/briefs" class="docs-link-primary">component briefs</a>, the
          <a href="/prompts#rubric" class="docs-link-primary">prompt rubric</a>,
          the
          <a href="/workshop#glossary" class="docs-link-primary">glossary</a>
          and the
          <a href="/troubleshooting#still-red" class="docs-link-primary"
            >rescue path</a
          >. Nobody continues red: if a step will not go green, use the rescue
          path, pair with a neighbour and tell the trainer right away.
        </p>
      </Callout>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section byo-block" aria-labelledby="block-00">
      <p class="byo-meta">09:00–09:30 · 30 min</p>
      <h2 class="docs-section-title" id="block-00">
        <span class="byo-num" aria-hidden="true">00</span> Choose your brief
      </h2>
      <p class="docs-step-desc">
        Pick one component: Toast, TagChip, StatCard or Avatar. Each
        <a href="/briefs" class="docs-link-primary">brief</a> says what a
        finished component owes: anatomy, axes, states and accessibility
        obligations. It does not say how to draw it; that is the exercise. Read
        the <a href="/briefs" class="docs-link-primary">shared rules</a> first,
        then your brief:
        <a href="/briefs/toast" class="docs-link-primary">Toast</a>,
        <a href="/briefs/tagchip" class="docs-link-primary">TagChip</a>,
        <a href="/briefs/statcard" class="docs-link-primary">StatCard</a>,
        <a href="/briefs/avatar" class="docs-link-primary">Avatar</a>.
      </p>
      <p class="docs-step-desc">
        You also meet the skills: a skill is a guide that Claude loads when the
        task matches. In your workspace that is <code>atelier-component</code>
        (the design-to-code loop for your workspace, which carries the day from
        Block 02 on), <code>uianatomy-mcp</code>,
        <code>figma-workspace-architect</code> (only in workspaces generated
        with Figma) and the four Storybook skills. See
        <a href="/agent-skills" class="docs-link-primary">Agent skills</a>.
      </p>
      <p class="docs-step-desc">
        Skipped the optional
        <a href="/first-component#bridge" class="docs-link-primary"
          >bridge exercise</a
        >
        (add a variant to your SettingsCard, with story and contract)? Do it now
        if there is time: Block 02 asks for exactly that trio, and it is cheaper
        to meet it on a component that already works.
      </p>
      <p class="byo-done">
        <strong>Done when</strong> you know your target component and your
        skills are installed.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section byo-block" aria-labelledby="block-01">
      <p class="byo-meta">09:30–11:00 · 90 min · high-risk block</p>
      <h2 class="docs-section-title" id="block-01">
        <span class="byo-num" aria-hidden="true">01</span> Design in Figma and
        write the handoff
      </h2>
      <p class="docs-step-desc">
        The handoff document is the prompt's input, so the quality of this block
        decides the rest of the day.
      </p>
      <ol class="byo-list byo-list--ordered">
        <li>
          Duplicate the Atelier file into your own drafts first, and never
          change the original file.
        </li>
        <li>
          Start from your brief's starter frame on the Workshop Templates page
          (it comes along when you duplicate): Toast / Starter, StatCard /
          Starter, TagChip / Starter or Avatar / Starter. Fills, and where the
          component has them paddings and radii, are already bound to Library
          Tokens variables, so the first step is copy and rename, not an empty
          canvas.
        </li>
        <li>
          Design in the Figma UI (about 40 min): layout, variants, token
          assignment. Then structure it through MCP (about 30 min): component
          properties and a11y audits. Plan about 5 min for the duplicate at the
          start and about 12 min for the handoff document plus a 3 min recap at
          the end.
        </li>
        <li>
          Aim for at least 2 variants × 2 states, properties, correct
          <code>--ui-*</code> tokens and a dark-mode variant. The exact matrix
          is in your brief.
        </li>
        <li>
          Before anyone prompts, write a short handoff document: draft URL and
          node id, chosen variants and states, token bindings, reuse versus
          rebuild, the behaviour from the brief, explicit exclusions, target
          files and acceptance criteria. It is a checklist, not a template.
          <a href="/design-to-code#handoff-example" class="docs-link-primary"
            >A filled handoff document</a
          >
          shows one.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <Callout tone="warning" title="If something fails">
        <p>
          Bridge not connected, node id does not resolve, port conflicts: see
          <a href="/troubleshooting" class="docs-link-primary"
            >Troubleshooting</a
          >
          and <a href="/figma-token" class="docs-link-primary">Figma access</a>.
          Still red: the
          <a href="/troubleshooting#still-red" class="docs-link-primary"
            >rescue path</a
          >.
        </p>
      </Callout>
      <p class="byo-done">
        <strong>Done when</strong> you have a master plus a filled handoff
        document.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section byo-block" aria-labelledby="block-02">
      <p class="byo-meta">11:15–12:30 · 75 min</p>
      <h2 class="docs-section-title" id="block-02">
        <span class="byo-num" aria-hidden="true">02</span> Contract, component,
        stories
      </h2>
      <p class="docs-step-desc">
        Prompt from the handoff document, not from the picture alone: behaviour
        and exclusions come from the document. Let Claude produce the contract,
        the component (with its test) and the stories in one step. One story per
        variant value and Boolean state, and a <code>play</code> for every
        behaviour line of the document.
      </p>
      <ol class="byo-list byo-list--ordered">
        <li>
          Start <code>npm run storybook</code> in parallel, then connect to it
          (use the port Storybook prints). This is deliberately local scope, so
          the shared <code>.mcp.json</code> stays untouched:
          <Code
            code={MCP_ADD}
            lang="shell"
            title="connect the local Storybook MCP"
          />
          Reconnect the client and check that the dev and test tools
          (<code>get-storybook-story-instructions</code>,
          <code>stories-preview</code>, <code>test-run</code>) are listed.
          Without this step they simply do not exist: "not available" means "not
          configured", not "server down".
          <code>get-storybook-story-instructions</code> is mandatory before
          every <code>*.stories.*</code> edit.
        </li>
        <li>
          The contract lives in your own workspace under
          <code>workshop-&lt;fw&gt;/src/contracts/</code>. The component
          declares its prop types directly; there is no separate spec interface
          file. See the
          <a href="/design-to-code#contract-example" class="docs-link-primary"
            >worked contract example</a
          >.
        </li>
        <li>
          Your Figma node needs a snapshot in your workspace. With the bridge
          connected, run the snapshot, then the check. Before the first run,
          replace the <code>&lt;YOUR_FIGMA_FILE_KEY&gt;</code> placeholder in
          the <code>figma:snapshot</code> script in <code>package.json</code>
          with your own file key if it is not already set:
          <Code
            code={SNAPSHOT_AND_CHECK}
            lang="shell"
            title="snapshot, then check"
          />
          Without a fresh snapshot your component is missing and the check
          reports <code>[NO-MASTER]</code> (a warning) instead of failing. Read
          every finding with the
          <a href="/design-to-code#finding-codes" class="docs-link-primary"
            >finding codes</a
          >.
        </li>
        <li>
          Later, for the parity check in Block 04, write the codeSpec fragment
          with:
          <Code code={EMIT} lang="shell" title="emit the codeSpec fragment" />
        </li>
        <li>
          Iterate until the prop table fits. Stories and the MCP docs are
          covered in <a href="/storybook" class="docs-link-primary">Storybook</a
          >.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p class="byo-done">
        <strong>Done when</strong> <code>check:contracts</code> is green
        offline.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section byo-block" aria-labelledby="block-03">
      <p class="byo-meta">13:30–15:00 · 90 min</p>
      <h2 class="docs-section-title" id="block-03">
        <span class="byo-num" aria-hidden="true">03</span> Iterate: prompt
        quality and the self-healing loop
      </h2>
      <p class="docs-step-desc">
        A single prompt is not the result. Write prompts, judge the output,
        correct it, and do not type the code yourself.
      </p>
      <ul class="byo-list">
        <li>
          Judge every prompt against the six-check
          <a href="/prompts#rubric" class="docs-link-primary">prompt rubric</a>
          before you send it. When a revision is needed, the failed check is the
          reason you state.
        </li>
        <li>
          Enforce token fidelity: ask for <code>--ui-*</code> custom properties.
        </li>
        <li>
          Run the test loop: <code>stories-preview</code> and
          <code>test-run</code> (with a11y) through the local Storybook MCP, or
          <code>nx storybook-test &lt;fw&gt;</code>, which runs every story in
          Chromium with play assertions and axe.
          <code>nx test &lt;lib&gt; --watch</code> covers only the unit half.
        </li>
        <li>
          Paste error messages back to Claude instead of debugging yourself. The
          loop is described in
          <a href="/design-to-code#self-healing-loop" class="docs-link-primary"
            >the self-healing loop</a
          >.
        </li>
        <li>
          The trainer hands out the golden prompts for this block on site.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="byo-done">
        <strong>Done when</strong> your stories are green and you revised the
        prompt at least once, with the reason stated.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section byo-block" aria-labelledby="block-04">
      <p class="byo-meta">15:15–16:30 · 75 min</p>
      <h2 class="docs-section-title" id="block-04">
        <span class="byo-num" aria-hidden="true">04</span> Verify: a11y, states,
        parity
      </h2>
      <p class="docs-step-desc">A quiet report is not a clean component.</p>
      <ol class="byo-list byo-list--ordered">
        <li>
          Three-step a11y check: <code>figma_audit_component_accessibility</code
          >, then the Storybook a11y panel (axe-core), then a keyboard
          walkthrough. Let Claude fix the findings. Background:
          <a href="/a11y-workflow#a11y-in-the-loop" class="docs-link-primary"
            >a11y in the loop</a
          >
          and
          <a href="/accessibility" class="docs-link-primary">Accessibility</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
          Check hover, active and disabled states, and the focus ring via
          tokens.
        </li>
        <li>
          Dark mode: use the theme control in the Storybook toolbar. It sets
          <code>data-theme</code>, exactly the selector <code>tokens.css</code>
          switches on. Backgrounds are canvas colours, not a theme. Note that
          axe runs only in light mode in <code>check:stories</code>: the toggle
          is a preview, not test coverage.
        </li>
        <li>
          Assemble your codeSpec from the fragment of Block 02
          (<code>componentAPI</code>, <code>metadata</code>, <code>tokens</code
          >). For accessibility, open the story in the running Storybook, copy
          the story root element's <code>outerHTML</code> in DevTools and pass
          it as <code>html</code> with <code>mapToCodeSpec: true</code> to
          <code>figma_scan_code_accessibility</code>.
        </li>
        <li>
          Run <code>figma_check_design_parity</code> on your own node. It needs
          the connected Desktop Bridge; without it the loop ends after
          <code>check:contracts</code> and <code>storybook-test</code>, which is
          a degraded run, not "just carry on". Try it once on purpose with an
          incomplete codeSpec, then falsify a spacing value deliberately: fields
          you did not pass are not compared. Read the result with
          <a href="/design-to-code#parity-report" class="docs-link-primary"
            >reading a parity report</a
          >.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p class="byo-done">
        <strong>Done when</strong> <code>storybook-test</code> with axe is green
        and every parity finding is triaged as code, Figma or contract.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section byo-block" aria-labelledby="block-05">
      <p class="byo-meta">16:40–17:30 · 50 min</p>
      <h2 class="docs-section-title" id="block-05">
        <span class="byo-num" aria-hidden="true">05</span> Show and tell, then
        transfer to your own design system
      </h2>
      <ul class="byo-list">
        <li>
          Cohorts have up to 8 participants, so each person gets strictly 3
          minutes. Show your component, one prompt that worked and one that
          failed.
        </li>
        <li>
          Transfer: run the self-check in
          <a href="/design-principles#check-your-own" class="docs-link-primary"
            >check your own system</a
          >
          on your own design system and pick one next step. The
          <a href="/readiness" class="docs-link-primary">readiness checklist</a>
          helps you judge a master from your own file.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="byo-done">
        <strong>Done when</strong> you leave with a next step for your own
        design system.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section" aria-labelledby="done">
      <h2 class="docs-section-title" id="done">Are you done?</h2>
      <p class="docs-step-desc">
        The end-of-day check, each item yours to verify. Every gate there is
        your own: red means red.
      </p>
      <ol class="byo-verify">
        {DAY2_DONE.map((item) => (
          <li set:html={inlineMd(item)} />
        ))}
      </ol>
    </section>

    <HelpFooter page="build-your-own" />

    <TrackNav />
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .byo-block {
    padding-top: 0.5rem;
  }
  .byo-meta {
    margin: 0 0 0.35rem;
    font-family: var(--font-mono, ui-monospace, monospace);
    font-size: var(--ui-font-size-sm);
    color: var(--ui-color-text-muted);
  }
  .byo-num {
    display: inline-block;
    margin-right: 0.35rem;
    font-family: var(--font-mono, ui-monospace, monospace);
    font-size: 0.7em;
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--ui-color-primary);
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ui-color-primary) 12%, transparent);
    padding: 0.1rem 0.45rem;
    border-radius: 3px;
    vertical-align: 0.1em;
  }
  .byo-list,
  .byo-verify {
    margin: 0.6rem 0 1rem;
    padding-left: 1.3rem;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    font-size: var(--ui-font-size-md);
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--ui-color-text);
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  .byo-list code,
  .byo-verify :global(code),
  .docs-step-desc code {
    font-family: var(--font-mono, ui-monospace, monospace);
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    color: var(--ui-color-primary);
    background: var(--ui-color-primary-light);
    padding: 0.05em 0.35em;
    border-radius: var(--ui-radius-sm);
  }
  .byo-done {
    margin: 1rem 0 0;
    padding: 0.6rem 0.9rem;
    border-left: 3px solid var(--ui-color-success);
    background: var(--ui-color-surface-raised);
    border-radius: 0 var(--ui-radius-md) var(--ui-radius-md) 0;
    font-size: var(--ui-font-size-md);
    line-height: 1.5;
  }
  .byo-done code {
    font-family: var(--font-mono, ui-monospace, monospace);
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    color: var(--ui-color-primary);
    background: var(--ui-color-primary-light);
    padding: 0.05em 0.35em;
    border-radius: var(--ui-radius-sm);
  }
</style>
